<InformationLayout title="About Elementa" description="Learn what Elementa offers, where its chemistry references come from, and how to contact its creator.">
  <p>
    Elementa is a free chemistry reference and study tool by
    <a href="https://www.smolkin.org">Michael Smolkin</a> at Smolk Apps.
    Explore <a href="/elements/">all 118 elements</a>,
    <a href="/compare/">compare their properties</a>, and use the
    <a href="/study/">study lab</a> for simplified atom diagrams, quizzes,
    printable worksheets, study notes and a saved element deck. No account is required.
  </p>
  <h2>Using the models and reference values</h2>
  <p>
    Electron-shell and orbital diagrams are teaching models. They don't show
    scale-accurate atoms or paths followed by electrons. Element pages link to
    references so you can check a value or explore it further, including
    <a href="https://ciaaw.org/atomic-weights.htm">IUPAC CIAAW</a>,
    <a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/atomic-spectra-database">NIST</a>,
    <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/periodic-table/">PubChem</a> and
    the <a href="https://periodic-table.rsc.org/">Royal Society of Chemistry</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check important values against the cited references and your course materials.
    Elementa isn't a laboratory safety guide, official exam resource or substitute
    for a teacher. The named reference organizations don't endorse this website.
  </p>
  <h2>Your study progress</h2>
  <p>
    Notes, quiz history and saved elements stay in this browser on this device.
    The <a href="/study/#/saved">Saved page</a> can export a JSON backup and import
    one again. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for the distinction
    between local study data and website usage analytics.
  </p>
  <h2>Source code and feedback</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://github.com/smolkapps/periodic-table-seo-element-pages">source code</a>
    is available under the MIT license. To report an error or suggest a change,
    email <a href="mailto:michael@smolkin.org">michael@smolkin.org</a>. Include the
    page or element and, for a factual correction, the reference you're using.
  </p>
</InformationLayout>
